#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "dad3d0a2-26e0-52c4-9e5a-abe6664cc02c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8616" :question/problem "A fair die has $9$ sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $9$. What is the probability of getting a number greater than or equal to $6$ on the next throw of the die?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number the die lands on. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}$, and we wish to compute\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 6)\n$$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim U \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}$, and the set\n\n$$\nS = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}\n$$\n\nhas\n\n$$\n\\mid S \\mid = 9-1 + 1 = 9\n$$\n\nitems. So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{9},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, …, 9 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nIn $S$, the items that satisfy\n\n$$\nX \\geq 6\n$$\n\nare $6, 7, 8, 9$. Since there are $4$ elements in total, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 6) = 4 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166687 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "de4ae41e-c981-5b5e-b52c-c8e7caf4c272" :question/math-academy-id "e-8647" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\n1. The amount of time, in seconds, needed to download a video.\n2. The number obtained when rolling a fair $4$-sided die whose sides are labeled with the numbers $5, 6, 7, 8$.\n3. The number of rainy days in a randomly selected summer month in New York City." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each given random variable.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because an amount of time is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of sides, and since the die is fair, each integer between $5$ and $8$ has an equal probability of being selected.\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because each possible number of rainy days is not equally likely. For example, it's more likely that there will be $5$ rainy days than $31$ rainy days.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2473833-c71c-5186-a12b-67e836950352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166287} {:answer/id #uuid "befa16f4-bc6b-5fcc-b6dd-c4610badece3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166288} {:answer/id #uuid "dbefadaf-3bb5-5096-8c7b-3d6a8d4522fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166289} {:answer/id #uuid "3b938956-23cc-55fc-b602-bb736ae6f44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166290} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff78f82-6395-50f5-b932-406234d8abcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166291}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc777b2-6887-5558-adfa-4aa52867398b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166286}] :question/id #uuid "200aef1f-7404-50e8-879d-3c216fa12872" :question/math-academy-id "q-97569" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\n1. The number obtained when picking an integer between $1$ and $10$ at random.\n2. The number obtained when picking a real number between $1$ and $10$ at random.\n3. The color of the ball obtained when a ball from a bag containing $3$ blue and $4$ red balls is chosen at random."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc3d191-cba3-52f4-a11c-dde3db0a56cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166293} {:answer/id #uuid "1af8addf-ed0d-5b82-873f-cf8c7cc8d138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166294} {:answer/id #uuid "21102de4-1d55-5a18-bc2c-327303bd4608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166295} {:answer/id #uuid "73dc79c3-61d5-5a44-b9d7-241c2f32a3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166296} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1fe4dc-1393-5f42-aead-cb877bdac66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166297}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2677ed19-d317-5fac-9ef2-ef42544595ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166292}] :question/id #uuid "29f6e184-5ce2-54d0-991f-fb9cc535577d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97691" :question/problem "In a videogame, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $100$ and $155$, inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability to occur, what is the probability of having less than $130$ obstacles?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "845c09e6-5d17-5bb5-adbc-3721e754fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166305} {:answer/id #uuid "b42d62fd-5de8-57e7-89e2-4a835c65eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166306} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bac43d-fbd2-555d-98fa-4f3cf841dda7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166307} {:answer/id #uuid "ece24b1c-2340-5941-9ac1-1dce184ab664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166308} {:answer/id #uuid "52958bf7-76a5-59bb-8124-ef40c1e1ee10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166309}] :answer-field/id #uuid "648b28e8-61ba-5183-92e3-64a3e48338c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166304}] :question/id #uuid "d3f94f8a-9ac9-561e-91b8-f484694ecc10" :question/math-academy-id "q-97745" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\n1. The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly.\n2. The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel.\n3. The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary."}]}
